course: history
lesson: 26
title: Explorers: around Africa and Columbus
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In the 1400s Europeans wanted spices, silk and gold from Asia, but the land routes were long and controlled by others, so sailors with new ships and the compass tried to get there by sea.
explain: Portuguese sailors crept down the coast of Africa in small, handy ships called caravels, mapping as they went, until Vasco da Gama reached India by sea in 1498.
explain: Christopher Columbus had another plan: sail west to reach Asia; he knew the Earth was round, like everyone educated, but he thought it was much smaller than it is, a bit like setting off to walk to the next town and finding a whole country in the way.
explain: In 1492 he reached the Caribbean, home to the Taíno and millions of other people, and plants, animals and diseases began to cross the Atlantic both ways.
explain: Long before him, Vikings had reached North America around the year 1000, and Chinese fleets under Zheng He had sailed as far as East Africa in the early 1400s.
fact: Europeans sailed into the unknown mostly to reach the spices, silk and gold of Asia by sea, because the land routes were long and controlled by others.
fact: Prince Henry of Portugal sent sailors down the coast of Africa in caravels, small handy ships, though he did not sail on these voyages himself.
fact: In 1498 Vasco da Gama sailed around the southern tip of Africa and reached India by sea, and Portugal grew rich from the spice trade.
fact: From 1405 to 1433 the Chinese admiral Zheng He led huge fleets across the Indian Ocean as far as East Africa.
fact: Christopher Columbus, from Genoa in Italy, believed he could reach Asia by sailing west, and Queen Isabella and King Ferdinand of Spain paid for his voyage.
fact: Educated people already knew the Earth was round; Columbus's mistake was thinking it was much smaller than it is.
fact: In 1492 Columbus sailed with three ships, the Niña, the Pinta and the Santa María, and after about five weeks from the Canary Islands reached an island in the Caribbean.
fact: Columbus thought he was near Asia, in the lands then called the Indies, so he called the people he met Indians.
fact: The first people Columbus met were the Taíno, who farmed, fished and made hammocks and canoes; within a few decades most of them had died from disease, forced labour and violence.
fact: Columbus was a brave, skilful sailor but also a harsh governor who enslaved native people, and many places now also mark Indigenous Peoples' Day.
fact: In the Columbian Exchange horses, cows and wheat went to the Americas, while potatoes, maize, tomatoes and chocolate came to Europe.
fact: The Americas are named after the Italian Amerigo Vespucci, who realised this was a New World and not Asia.
fact: In 1494 Spain and Portugal agreed a line on the map, the Treaty of Tordesillas, splitting new lands between them without asking the people who lived there, which is why Brazil speaks Portuguese.
exercise: What were the explorers mainly looking for? | A sea route to the spices, silk and gold of Asia | A place to build castles and forts on new islands | Ice for their drinks | The edge of the flat Earth
exercise: What was a caravel? | A small, handy sailing ship | A Portuguese castle on the coast of Africa | A kind of spice | A camel caravan
exercise: Which way did Vasco da Gama sail to reach India? | Around the southern tip of Africa | Across the Pacific Ocean, sailing east | Over the North Pole through the ice | Through the Sahara by boat
exercise: In which year did Vasco da Gama reach India? | 1498 | 1066 | 1776 | 1914
exercise: Who led huge Chinese fleets as far as East Africa? | Zheng He | Kublai Khan | Ibn Battuta | Marco Polo
exercise: Who paid for Columbus's voyage? | The king and queen of Spain | The king of England and his lords | The Pope alone | The Ottoman sultan
exercise: What was Columbus wrong about? | The size of the Earth | The shape of the Earth | Which way was west | Whether the sea was salty
exercise: What were the names of Columbus's three ships? | Niña, Pinta and Santa María | Victoria, Trinidad and Concepción | Endeavour, Resolution and Discovery | Mayflower, Speedwell and Rose
exercise: Why did Columbus call the people he met Indians? | He thought he was near the Indies in Asia | The people on the beach told him they came from India | They asked him to | It was a Taíno word
exercise: Who were the first people Columbus met? | The Taíno | The Aztecs | The Māori people | The Inca of Peru
exercise: What happened to the Taíno after Columbus arrived? | Most died within decades from disease, forced labour and violence | They conquered Spain | Nothing much changed for them at all for centuries | They all moved to Europe
exercise: Name a food that travelled from America to Europe. | Potatoes | Wheat | Rice | Apples
exercise: Which animal was brought from Europe to the Americas? | The horse | The llama of the Andes | The wild turkey | The spotted jaguar
exercise: Who were the Americas named after? | Amerigo Vespucci | Christopher Columbus | Vasco da Gama | Queen Isabella
exercise: Why do people in Brazil speak Portuguese? | A 1494 treaty gave that land to Portugal | Brazilians invented Portuguese and taught it to Portugal | Columbus was Portuguese | Spain sold Brazil in 1950
test: teacher in a cartoon says columbus proved the earth was round. fix it | Educated people already knew; he was wrong about its size | Yes, he proved it in 1492 | No, he proved it was flat | No, Magellan proved it was square, and Columbus agreed
test: Your pizza has tomato sauce. What would an Italian pizza in 1400 have lacked? | Tomatoes, which came from America | Bread, which came from China | Cheese, which came from Australia | Salt, which came from Canada
test: A book says Columbus discovered an empty land. What is wrong with that? | Millions of people already lived there | Nothing, it was empty land with only animals living there | He landed in Asia | It was full of Vikings only
test: Can someone be both brave and cruel? Which fits Columbus? | A skilful sailor and also a harsh governor | Only a hero with no faults at all, kind to everyone he met | Only a villain who could not sail | Neither, he never sailed
test: If the Earth really were as small as Columbus thought, where would he have landed? | In Asia, as he hoped | In Antarctica | On the Moon | Back in Spain the next day
test: lads say the Irish always had potatoes, its like their thing. true? | No, potatoes came from South America after 1492 | Yes, potatoes grew in Ireland since the Ice Age | Yes, Vikings brought them from Norway | No, the Irish never ate potatoes
test: Why did Portugal and Spain think they could split the world on a map? | They acted without asking the people who lived there | Everyone in the world had voted for it in Rome | The Pope lived in Brazil | The lands were empty
test: Who reached the Americas long before Columbus, apart from the people living there? | Vikings, around the year 1000 | The Romans, in wooden ships with oars | The Mongols, by horse | Nobody at all
test: Why would a new sea route to India make Portugal rich? | It could buy spices directly and sell them in Europe | Spices were free in Portugal for everyone | India paid Portugal to visit with gold every year | Ships could carry gold mines
test: How long did Columbus sail from the Canary Islands to the Caribbean in 1492? | About five weeks | About five years | About five short hours | About five days only
exam: Identify the principal error in the calculations of Christopher Columbus. | He greatly underestimated the size of the Earth | He believed the Earth to be flat and that ships could fall off | He mistook west for east | He miscounted his ships
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the voyage of Vasco da Gama in fourteen hundred and ninety-eight? | He reached India by sea around the southern extremity of Africa | He crossed the Pacific Ocean and returned by way of the Arctic seas | He discovered the Strait of Magellan | He reached China overland
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Treaty of Tordesillas of fourteen hundred and ninety-four. | It divided newly reached lands between Spain and Portugal without consulting their inhabitants | It ended the Hundred Years' War | It granted the Americas to England | It was agreed by the Taíno
exam: Ascertain which of the following was conveyed from the Americas to Europe in the course of the Columbian Exchange. | Maize | Wheat and barley | Horses | Cattle
